 {"id": "TJ-DF-054", "title": "[P3] Resident bridge serializes concurrent clients: 2 clients x 5 queries each take 53-59ms wall vs ~15ms serial \u2014 single-threaded accept loop queues concurrent verdicts (verified live, dev host)", "status": "complete", "priority": "P3", "created_at": "2026-10-23T04:50:00-05:00", "created_by": "dogfood-tick-2026-10-23", "depends_on": [], "blocks": [], "capability_tags": ["bridge", "resident", "concurrency"], "complexity": 2, "attempts": 0, "blocked_reason": null, "description": "Dogfood run 22. Two threads each issued 5 sequential 'ls -la' verdicts against ONE daemon: every reply was OK (no errors, no resets) but per-verdict latency rose from ~5ms solo to 9.3-15.2ms and wall was 58.8ms for 10 verdicts vs 2 threads x ~5ms x 5 = ~25ms if concurrent. The daemon accepts one connection at a time (matches its serve() design; no thread/process spawn in interruptor_resident.py). For the README's stated 'hundreds of verdicts per minute' busy-agent case this is likely fine in practice, but the behavior is undocumented \u2014 an integrator expecting per-client parallelism gets silent serialization. FIX: document single-client-at-a-time semantics in README (one line in the resident table row), or add a note that concurrent clients queue.", "reasoning": "Evidence: /tmp/df-tj-1023/conc_probe.py output (2x5, all OK, wall 58.8ms, per-Q 5.3-15.2ms vs solo p50 5.11ms); /tmp/df-tj-1023/perf_rtt2.py (solo numbers).", "acceptance": "1. The resident transport's single-client semantics are documented: `sed -n '346,412p' README.md | grep -Eiq 'one client at a time|single client|seriali(z|s)ed|clients queue|queue behind'` exits 0 (today that range has no such wording, exit 1).\n2. The documented behaviour is reproducible: with the daemon serving at /tmp/tj-conc.sock, `printf '%s\\n' 1 2 3 4 5 | xargs -P2 -n1 env TERMINAL_JAIL_BRIDGE_SOCK=/tmp/tj-conc.sock python3 plugin/terminal_jail/interruptor_resident.py --client --command \"ls -la\" | grep -c '\"rule_id\": \"allow-ls\"'` prints `5` \u2014 two concurrent clients are both served every verdict, so the note describes queueing, not breakage.", "commit_hash": "6847c9b", "worker_status": "complete", "worker_summary": "Wave recovery: work already merged to main (verified via merge-base). Closing row with actual commit hash."}
